WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.990
- Khan Academy, welcome to
The Daily Homeroom livestream,

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.940
for those of y'all that
this is your first time.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.740
This is, really just a way
for us to stay connected

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.410
during school closures.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.900
Obviously, Khan Academy
we have many resources

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.290
for students, teachers and
parents to be able to work

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.700
whether the school is
in session physically,

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.530
or, whether it's happening at home,

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:24.460
as I think most of y'all
are now having to cope with.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:27.280
And we realize, above and
beyond those resources,

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.970
in math and English and
language arts, and science

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.610
and humanities that go
from pre-K through college,

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:35.360
that we need to provide more support.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.640
It's our duty as a not
for profit with a mission

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.660
to provide a free world
class education for anyone,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.450
anywhere to step up right
now in this time of crisis.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.710
And so that's why we're
doing teacher webinars,

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.750
parent webinars, this
daily homeroom live stream

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.750
for everyone as well as, things
like, the daily schedules

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.130
that many of y'all are now leveraging.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:56.570
To think about how to structure your days,

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.350
for students of different age groups.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.090
I will remind everyone as I always do,

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.160
that we are a not for profit organization.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.600
And, we are supportive, the
only reason we can exist

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.410
is because of philanthropic donations

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:11.970
from folks like yourself.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.270
And even before this crisis,
we were operating at a deficit

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.440
and now during this
crisis, our server traffic

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.990
is up two and a half,
approaching three times

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:24.400
our registrations from
students and parents,

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.420
are actually students and
teachers are six fold,

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.090
parent registrations are 20 fold.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.420
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.270
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.310
I do wanna give a special shout
out to several corporations

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.690
who stepped up in record
time when they realized

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:40.820
that we needed help
and not just generally,

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.810
but especially in this
COVID response period.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.380
Bank of America stepped
up in that first weekend,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:51.150
followed by AT&amp;T,
Google.org and now Novartis,

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.070
this has been incredible help.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.250
Any of you who are corporate leaders

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.920
or work for corporations,
we need more help.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.850
So I would love to add logos to this.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.760
And for all of you individuals out there,

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.960
we still are running at
a significant deficit.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.940
So every donation matters,
whether it's as small

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.260
as the $3 donation, $5, $10.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:10.822
We know that that is hard earned money

00:02:10.822 --> 00:02:13.770
and, we really appreciate
it, but we really do need it,

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.200
if we wanna support kids around the world,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.740
and parents and teachers.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:22.360
So today we have a fairly
exciting live stream.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.680
We have a clinical
psychologist, David Anderson.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.150
I encourage everyone,
I say this every time

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:31.464
to start putting questions
on Facebook and YouTube

00:02:31.464 --> 00:02:34.600
that you might have for
either our guest, Dave

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.730
or myself about anything,

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:38.430
but we're gonna talk about something

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.870
that is very front of mind
for a lot of folks right now.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:43.610
We are all socially distanced.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.370
Many of us have kids at home,

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.270
kids are trying to stay learning,

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.360
but there's many other
things that are going on

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.690
in all of our minds.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.510
Anxiety is up, stress is up for everyone.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.850
And I'm hoping Dave can help us navigate.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.010
And I'm speaking not
just as a representative

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.290
of the Khan Academy community.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:04.290
I'm also speaking as a
parent who has seen some of,

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.690
some of our, some of the difficulties

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:07.720
of what we're going through.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.940
So thanks for joining us, Dave.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:11.520
And you know, maybe the first question

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.010
is any general advice you have for parents

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.810
and students who are just trying

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.230
to cope with everything
going on right now.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.170
- Right, well, look, thanks for having me.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.530
It's wonderful to be able to be here

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:24.870
and to have a forum to kind of talk

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.150
about some of these things.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.540
You know, we have a
practice on both coasts,

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:31.410
about 60 clinicians in
Child Mind Institute,

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.510
and we're hearing a lot
from parents and students.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.540
I would say that, you
know, the first things

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:37.830
that are the top of mind,

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:40.990
especially for parents are
to manage expectations.

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:42.840
I mean, the biggest
thing is that none of us

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.470
have encountered challenges
like this before.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.654
And I count myself within
that with two children.

00:03:47.654 --> 00:03:50.630
You know, and something
we're trying to manage

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.130
the work lives that we
were, attempted managed

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:57.020
before this crisis hit with childcare

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:59.800
and without many of the
major supports that we have,

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.170
you know, a time of
online schooling is tough.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:04.510
It's gonna be tough for anybody.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:05.830
So, some of the first things we talked

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.850
to parents about are taking it to do lists

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:09.470
and that kind of level of perfectionism

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:11.570
they would normally expect of themselves.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:13.320
And really trying to
edit those things down

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.290
as much as possible to ensure

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:17.690
that there's something
manageable each day.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.310
And you have a moment to
kind of mindfully pause

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.750
and perhaps enjoy some
of the silver linings

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.170
of this in terms of time spent with family

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.570
and an opportunity to
connect a little bit more.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.445
- And what should we keep a lookout on,

00:04:31.445 --> 00:04:33.740
for our children or even for ourselves?

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.790
I know you focus on children especially,

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.520
but as a just a clinical
psychologist generally,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.280
you know, I think sometimes you can,

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.680
things can happen in bits and pieces

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.660
and you know, you've gone too
far before and it's too late.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:45.980
And, what should we look out for,

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.070
signs of difficulty in our
kids or even ourselves.

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:50.380
- I mean the reality to your point,

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:52.580
is we always say this
thing that child therapy

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.640
is so much about it being
mediated by the parents,

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.200
and parents aren't necessarily

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.460
the cause of the child's
particular difficulties,

00:04:58.460 --> 00:04:59.700
but they're often the solution.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.130
So we spent, probably
about a half the time

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.600
spending talking to
parents even when the child

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.010
might be the particular
focus of our intervention

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:12.210
and you know, in terms of
how parents can support kids

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.180
at the moment and what we can,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.530
we can help them to think about,

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:16.760
especially with online schooling,

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:18.673
cause I'll go back to one
of your earlier questions.

00:05:18.673 --> 00:05:22.210
It's that, you know,
we're, everybody's adapting

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:24.690
this kind of new normal
like, schools are trying

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.940
to outline assignments in certain ways

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.750
and make it so that, it's
manageable for the kids

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:34.160
and they may not necessarily
be getting data in real time

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.370
from parents related to
what the kids can handle

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.040
and what kind of scaffolding they need.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:39.510
I think the kids we're
especially concerned

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.340
about at the moment, for
our clinical population

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:44.050
are those with mental health
and learning disorders

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:47.930
that normally are supported
in allowing them access

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:49.830
to a school curriculum in school,

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:51.450
but which, you know, we may see

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:54.240
a lack of those kinds of support services

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.010
or where parents are being
asked to be therapists,

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.770
speech language pathologist
and teacher all in one.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.140
And that's something that's exceedingly

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.810
difficult for everybody right now.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:04.500
- And how are you all
supporting those parents

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.570
or supporting those students?

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:10.620
- Yeah, a lot of it is,
retooling our clinical services

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.070
to be digital at this time.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:16.620
So in many ways we're starting
from a kind of population

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.820
focused prevention level,
in that Child Mind Institute

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:22.393
now has two Facebook lives per day.

00:06:25.695 --> 00:06:28.440
I did a section on the
website, which you can see here

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.520
on Coronavirus support and resources

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.600
just for supporting families and includes

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.190
links to our daily tips
and Facebook lives.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:39.560
A link to phone consultations
that we can provide right now

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.320
for families that are having difficulty.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.820
And then also articles
that are really focused

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.560
on coping with Coronavirus crisis.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.170
We've tried to make those
articles targeted to age groups,

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:52.290
to parents stress management,
incorporation of mindfulness,

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:54.730
and then specific diagnostic clusters

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:56.700
where parents may be experiencing

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.980
a certain kind of unique difficulties,

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:00.840
say for kids on the autism spectrum

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.820
or kids who might have
difficulty with behavior.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:07.150
So I thank you for scrolling
through that section

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:08.720
of our website right there.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:11.090
You can also, I'll be
doing these Facebook lives

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:13.593
twice next week, so if
folks have questions

00:07:13.593 --> 00:07:15.750
that go unanswered
between you and me, Sal,

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:17.900
they can catch me again
a little bit later.

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:19.890
- No, this is super useful.

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:21.930
And then we're getting a
lot of interesting questions

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.280
here for you Dave.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:24.750
This first one is from YouTube.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.150
Molly Franklin asks, as an extrovert,

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.010
how can I keep from losing
my mind during quarantine?

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:30.860
Exclamation mark.

00:07:32.593 --> 00:07:34.150
- That's a really good question.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:36.650
I mean, you know, I
think that in many ways

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:40.050
we've seen an explosion of
these kinds of self categorizing

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.430
terms on Twitter and on
the internet right now,

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:45.110
where folks realize like
in terms of the definition

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.650
of extrovert, we see it
operationalized right now

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:50.800
and on the internet as somebody
who really gets their energy

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:52.750
and really feels like
their mood gets a boost

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.830
in any given day, from the
contact, the close contact

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.010
in person that they
have with other people,

00:07:58.010 --> 00:07:59.500
that's where they get their energy.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.210
And so look, what we try to do at first

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:06.180
is help extroverts to, cope
in a sort of accepting way,

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:09.410
in that, we know that
it's not gonna be the best

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:11.880
in terms of how to support
their mental health right now.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:14.610
We know that a lot of the ways

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:16.490
that they would normally draw energy

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:19.230
or get a mood boost are
not available to them.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:21.500
So it's about thinking as
creatively as possible.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.060
We try to assess with people,

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:25.110
what the level of
relationship is with anybody

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.250
they might currently be
socially distancing with,

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:29.050
how they can make the most

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:32.240
of those safe in-person connections.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.240
And then similarly how they
can connect with others

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.510
to just do daily activities
kind of together,

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:39.970
in the sense that can you
watch a TV show together?

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:42.460
Are there online games
you can connect with

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:43.750
and play with other people?

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.730
And I'll give one last,
quite creative suggestion

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:49.280
I've seen from some of
the teenagers I treat,

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:51.470
which is social distancing walks,

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.370
in a sense that they'll
meet up across a block

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:57.770
from each other, never be on
the same side of the street,

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:00.930
but at least feel like
they're walking together

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.330
and can look at each
other, you know, in person.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:06.640
- That's fascinating cause
I think the rational part

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.120
of our brain says, why does it matter

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:09.540
if we're watching that, you know

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:11.580
that Netflix show at the same time.

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:13.310
But to your point there is something

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:14.660
deep inside of us that says,

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:17.430
Oh, other people are
experiencing it at the same time.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:19.850
- And that's the thing, I'm
deep into cheer right now.

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:21.290
Next up is Tiger King.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:23.910
But I think for all of us, it's that,

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.850
you know, if we don't have a partner

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:27.900
or another family member to watch with,

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.850
it becomes a question of
can we watch with a friend

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:35.200
or, you know, do this over
some sort of online forum

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.270
where somebody is on
FaceTime at the same time

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:40.970
as we're watching on television

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:42.920
and we're watching in real time together.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:45.410
It at least gives some
sense of shared experience,

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:48.297
particularly for extroverts
really feed off that.

00:09:48.297 --> 00:09:50.120
We even see people
- And that's some of the logic

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:51.790
- Cook the same food together.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:53.400
Sorry, yeah.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:54.233
- Oh, that's cool.

00:09:54.233 --> 00:09:55.597
Cook the same food and then share a meal

00:09:55.597 --> 00:09:57.960
that's a neat idea.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.870
- Exactly, and you get
to compare, you know,

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:01.460
who did it best and you know,

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:03.120
kind of tinker with each other's recipes.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.100
There's a lot that can happen with that,

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:08.064
especially if your friend is
a better chef than you are.

00:10:08.064 --> 00:10:09.375
- (laughs) Yeah.

00:10:09.375 --> 00:10:11.280
Although, you could
pretend that your own dish

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:13.980
was the more delicious,
no one will ever know.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:17.140
But, this is somewhat
the logic you can always,

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:21.140
Obviously we could do video
versions of this live stream.

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:22.210
And then it might even, you know,

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:23.770
we wouldn't have our
little technical glitches

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:26.569
that sometimes we have
with our, our broadband.

00:10:26.569 --> 00:10:29.420
- Sorry (speaking off mic)

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.842
- No one should feel
broadband guilt at this point.

00:10:31.842 --> 00:10:33.150
(laughs)

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:36.050
But, this is the value, that
we're all globally right now

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:37.180
having this shared experience.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:39.780
And we're getting a ton of
more questions from Facebook.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:44.010
Taylor Cobian asks, how do
you cope with school work load

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:45.850
without getting distracted?

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.710
Personally, as a high school student,

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:49.230
I'm struggling with staying focused

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:51.853
since I have ADHD, advice.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:55.150
- So my first job at
the Child Mind Institute

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:57.480
was running our clinical
center for kids with ADHD

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.330
and behavioral issues.

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:01.630
So this is, this is right
in my clinical wheelhouse.

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:03.370
So thank you for the softball.

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:05.340
I mean, look, with a lot of the patients

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.696
that we're working with
with ADHD right now,

00:11:07.696 --> 00:11:10.260
you know, we know certain things,

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:14.250
we know that if they get
sleep, focus can be better.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:15.420
If they take medicine,

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:17.220
it's important to think about the timing

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.950
and when they're gonna get
the most benefit from that.

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:20.960
And then beyond that,

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:23.410
it's important to think
about organizational skills

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.230
and the structuring of the day.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.010
So with a lot of my patients right now,

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:29.310
we'll think about,
especially with adolescents,

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:31.560
even though they may be
waking up slightly earlier

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:34.290
than they were say, during the school day,

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.150
we're structuring their workspace,

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:38.500
to make sure it's as free
of distractions as we can.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:40.350
Maybe putting any distracting devices

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:42.400
where friends might be
trying to contact us

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.650
outside the door or
elsewhere in the house.

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:47.040
We're trying to get good
work blocks in the day.

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:49.290
So trying to make sure we've
got at least one work block

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.330
before lunch and perhaps starting

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:53.210
with some of the more ambitious tasks,

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:55.670
that we know will have less
energy to really tackle

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:58.190
or focus, to really
tackle later in the day.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:00.670
Structuring good meals and breaks

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.010
and really trying to not save
a lot of the challenging work.

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:06.610
More like the easy stuff
you can kind of knock off,

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:09.700
for the late nighttime
either when medicine

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:12.210
or motivation has really worn off

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:14.140
and it's about kind of, you know,

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:15.730
managing yourself like that.

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:18.050
And then if you can't
talk back to yourself

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:20.790
to combat procrastination effectively,

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:23.650
sometimes we've seen teens
and college students,

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:25.820
really try to get accountability buddies,

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:27.710
who can kind of keep them on task and say,

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:29.860
all right, let me meet
with you for five minutes

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:30.920
at the beginning of the day.

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:32.070
What's your list?

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:34.280
And then, you know, how can
we make sure we check in,

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:37.513
in a non-judgmental way
to keep you on track.

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:39.490
- That's super powerful.

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:41.190
A lot of what you described, once again,

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:42.690
the rational parts of our brains, like,

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:44.880
Oh, why should I have
to text message a friend

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:48.240
when I've checked something off
of my, off of my to do list?

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:50.380
Or why should I have to eat
a, you know, watch a movie.

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:52.340
But there's, at the same
time there's someone else.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:54.050
But there's something
very powerful about having

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:57.450
that community accountability
to what you're doing.

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:59.395
And we've talked about it
at previous live streams,

00:12:59.395 --> 00:13:02.330
just, just getting started,
having those early wins,

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:03.930
but do some of the high
cognitive little things

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:06.274
early with breaks,
that's super good advice.

00:13:06.274 --> 00:13:08.630
- And it's really also,
that friends can help us

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.210
be accountable in a
more non-judgmental way.

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:13.040
Not to mention friends are often the ones

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:14.650
who are the most gentle with us

00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:16.650
and helping us to be self-compassionate

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:19.000
and also to realize we deserve breaks.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:20.610
Even if we weren't as efficient

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:22.763
in a given day as we expected we would be.

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:24.950
- You have better friends than I do.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:26.769
Mine are quite, no, I'm kidding.

00:13:26.769 --> 00:13:28.559
(laughter)

00:13:28.559 --> 00:13:31.053
- You choose the accountability
buddies carefully.

00:13:31.053 --> 00:13:33.320
- Yes, you should see some
of the texts accountability

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:36.110
that I have with my friends
when we have one group,

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:38.750
where we text each other
whenever we eat something

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:42.460
we should not and, they are
not, they're quite harsh.

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:44.133
We are all harsh to each other.

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.120
- I've seen folks do
things where their friend

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:50.420
will control a certain
portion of their money

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:52.900
and it will be donated to
a cause they don't support,

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:55.020
if they you know, don't
accomplish their goals,

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:56.810
for to a cause they could do support.

00:13:56.810 --> 00:13:59.930
If it does, you know you can
set up lots of different,

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:02.940
you know, schemes for
yourself with your friends.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:03.900
- That's powerful.

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.170
So, this next question is from YouTube.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:07.480
And actually this is
something where we could

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:08.800
probably both take a stab at answering it.

