00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:39.447
Even pre-crisis.

00:15:39.447 --> 00:15:41.610
You know, I really have to thank my wife

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:43.447
and my mother-in-law for stepping up,

00:15:43.447 --> 00:15:45.020
and, and filling in the gap.

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:47.450
And, my wife is a
physician, a rheumatologist.

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:49.300
So you can imagine,
there's a lot of complexity

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:50.370
in her world as well.

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:53.390
So she's been kind of the
hero of the household lately,

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:55.080
but it's tough on everyone.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:57.310
And I think we all just have to, you know,

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:59.410
take one step at a time
and to quote Frozen II,

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:01.300
do the next right thing.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:03.240
Dave, I'd love to hear personally,

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:06.260
how, how are you coping and
your family and your work?

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:08.390
- I just thought my son's
favorite character is Elsa.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:12.160
So if I can mix flexibility,
gratitude and frozen references

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:15.260
into my speech here,
you know, I think that,

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:16.453
that was pretty perfect.

00:16:16.453 --> 00:16:19.540
Now, you know, I think
that it at Child Mind,

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:22.500
you know, we have, 300 employees
across a couple of coasts

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:26.390
and similar to you, there
were aspects of our work,

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:30.590
that were, as a nonprofit
that were already online

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:33.330
and, you know, fruitful for collaboration

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:35.205
even when we're working at distance.

00:16:35.205 --> 00:16:39.040
I think others like clinical
care, have both suffered

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:42.140
and then also been adapted
in new and inventive ways

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:45.640
to serve our patients in
the sense that we all knew

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:47.980
that the models of treatment
that we use for children,

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:52.980
adolescents and families,
are most tested in person,

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:55.240
but, in an environment like this,

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:59.000
in many ways, we've seen
organizations like ourselves,

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:02.360
kind of figure out how
we can become borderless,

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:06.290
in that we can serve with
consultations or clinical care

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:09.820
and really creative ways
using video camera coaching

00:17:09.820 --> 00:17:12.730
with parents at home or all
kinds of different formats

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:16.510
online just to get in
touch with our patients

00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:18.600
and to really make sure
we can provide support

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:21.050
as best we can during this time.

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:25.360
Like you, I have a three year
old and a seven month old

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:28.220
at home and the only way that
I've been able to function

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:32.220
or keep work up is because
of my wonderful wife

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:35.000
and in-laws who also live with us.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:37.700
But even with that said, my son is used to

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:40.500
a bounded lifestyle, my
son, my three-year-old

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:43.670
and where daddy goes to
work each day in an office

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:45.610
and he goes to his pre-school.

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:47.450
And the fact that I'm working upstairs

00:17:47.450 --> 00:17:49.360
has been a real adjustment period for him,

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:51.500
in the sense that he
doesn't really understand

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:53.540
why he can't be involved in everything.

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:55.080
And so I've tried to work out a rhythm,

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:56.740
where I take a long lunch break

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:59.320
within each day outside in the yard.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:02.040
We need, you know, have at least a meeting

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:04.350
with a team member of mine, not a patient

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.740
that he joins each day.

00:18:05.740 --> 00:18:07.680
And then I just try to drop everything,

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:09.290
the second the workday is over,

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:10.750
and spend a good bit of time with him.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:12.490
But I think everybody's trying to find

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:14.300
their balance that way.

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:16.722
- You know, whenever I start
to feel sorry for ourselves

00:18:16.722 --> 00:18:18.990
because of my youngest is five years old

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:21.130
and he's a little bit difficult sometimes,

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:24.040
I remind myself of those of
you who have a three year old

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:26.030
or a seven month old at home.

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:29.580
So, my sympathies, that's hard.

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:30.990
- The funniest thing is that, you know,

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:32.417
in the midst of this,
I think my wife and I,

00:18:32.417 --> 00:18:34.380
had so many goals for, you know,

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:36.400
where we'd like him to be right now.

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:38.520
Like, you know, we've gotten
through potty training

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:42.360
the last year, like he's
sleeping in his own, big kid bed,

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:46.280
and, just in the last few
nights, in the last week or so,

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:48.580
he's found it unable to
sleep through the night

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:50.220
without one of us in his bed.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:51.570
And we're just adapting to that.

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:54.060
Like, I think in the
normal rhythm of life,

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:54.910
we'd say to ourselves,

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:56.940
no, we've got to set some boundaries here.

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:00.034
But the reality is our kids,
we're trying to stay healthy.

00:19:00.034 --> 00:19:02.970
We're trying to keep everybody
safe and their mood up.

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:05.830
So, you know, what's a little
bit of relaxing of boundaries

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:08.910
right now if it makes
everybody feel safe and loved.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:09.990
- Well, that's actually a good segue

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:12.300
to this next question that I have here.

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:14.130
And this is, you know,
the screen time question

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:15.180
has always been a question,

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:18.080
but obviously a lot of us
are relying on screen time,

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:20.620
both socially and
academically and work-wise.

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:25.620
So from Facebook, Samson
Senate Senateda or Sign Thado,

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:28.340
I thought it was a really
assigned data trick,

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:30.380
tricking the metric username, I like that.

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:34.500
Well, Sam sign sign data, is
it good for kids to go online

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:37.040
for three hours in the
morning even with breaks?

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:39.922
Some kids complain of
headaches and bleary eyes.

00:19:39.922 --> 00:19:40.990
Yeah, I'd love your, you know,

00:19:40.990 --> 00:19:43.850
there's the pre-COVID screen time debate

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:48.230
and now there's the current
COVID screen time debate.

00:19:48.230 --> 00:19:52.610
- I just did a webinar for
parents on this last week,

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:55.250
where we talked about how pre-COVID,

00:19:55.250 --> 00:19:57.280
the checklist was, you know,

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:59.450
in terms of stressing out
over screen time balance,

00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:01.280
we ask parents, let's check off

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:02.740
a number of developmental boxes.

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:04.730
And as long as you've got those things,

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:06.713
you may not have to worry
about how much free time

00:20:06.713 --> 00:20:08.650
your child is spending on screens.

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:11.040
As long as you're kind
of monitoring content

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:12.423
and the interactions.

00:20:12.423 --> 00:20:14.410
And we would check off, you know,

00:20:14.410 --> 00:20:16.200
are they invested in school,

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.290
do they have extracurricular activities?

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:20.170
Do you have some family time,

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:22.850
you know, do they have face
to face contact with friends

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:24.170
and are they getting enough sleep?

00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:26.290
Those would be some of the
things on the checklist.

00:20:26.290 --> 00:20:28.330
The way it's switched for COVID

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:31.150
is around basic wellness practices

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:33.740
and then the fact that the
balance has really shifted,

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:36.040
in that we're asking parents,

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:38.730
okay, is everybody in the house eating?

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:39.980
Do you know where your
food is coming from?

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:42.680
Let's check like kind of
hierarchy of needs type things.

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:45.900
You know, do you feel like
everyone's getting enough sleep?

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:47.460
How can we help with that?

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:50.430
Are people getting exercise in some form?

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:53.040
Are you finding ways
to give yourself breaks

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:56.240
above and beyond school
or work responsibilities?

00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:58.430
And you know, it's, it's this question

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:01.420
of once you've kind of
checked off those boxes

00:21:01.420 --> 00:21:05.660
of finding just ways to
engage in basic routines

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:07.920
of wellness over the course of the day.

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:11.060
We're trying to get people to
really think about relaxing,

00:21:11.060 --> 00:21:13.570
that, you know, if you
wanna focus on say an hour

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:16.240
or two a day to find
screen free activities,

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:19.224
destruction for kids,
congratulate yourself

00:21:19.224 --> 00:21:21.810
and then you know, monitor the content

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:23.640
and monitor what they're
watching of course.

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:26.410
But it's okay to relax the
boundaries during this time

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:28.080
and we'll snap them back over the summer

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.990
when everybody can be maybe outside

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:33.270
and hopefully when we're
through this to some degree.

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:35.150
- Right, it's an interesting struggle

00:21:35.150 --> 00:21:36.820
cause we're always, you
know, we always feel guilty

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:38.250
to some degree, I mean
there's good screen time

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:40.522
and not so good screen time
or neutral screen time,

00:21:40.522 --> 00:21:42.950
but obviously we're
all leaning on it more.

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:45.550
But it's your point, all
of our own mental health,

00:21:45.550 --> 00:21:48.960
we all need actually might,
might need some screen time

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:50.431
to connect with folks, to learn.

00:21:50.431 --> 00:21:53.570
But it also, if it, if it
can help offload a parent

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:56.250
for even half an hour, an
hour and give them a break,

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:58.880
if their stress level goes
down, it helps the student.

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:01.460
- And this goes back, to the
American Academy of Pediatrics

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:03.220
recommendations regarding screen time,

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:05.490
where, you know, they're conservative

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:07.850
but at the same time they
really focus on the idea

00:22:07.850 --> 00:22:10.800
that mediated viewing with a child,

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:12.880
in a sense that you watch
something with the kid

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:16.470
and talk about it or educational
high quality programming.

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:17.640
This is gonna sound like a plant,

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:19.690
but many of my patients and families

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:22.860
are watching Khan Academy,
you know, as part of this

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:25.413
because you know, in that
sense there are parents

00:22:25.413 --> 00:22:28.300
that have said, look, if my
kids are gonna be on screens

00:22:28.300 --> 00:22:30.900
and that's gonna be how
they're gonna occupy themselves

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:34.400
through this crisis, I may as
well make some good choices

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:36.490
about what I'm exposing them to.

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:38.220
And there'll be some time that I give them

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:39.680
where they choose it.

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:41.370
And then other times where I say,

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:44.640
here's a forum that I know
is all high quality content,

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:48.200
choose from this and occupy
thyself with something,

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:50.680
you know, like the boxer
rebellion I saw framed,

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:53.000
you know, on your white or
your chalkboard behind you,

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:54.000
that's a great thing

00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:56.950
- That has been in my to do for a while.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:59.303
I still have not made the boxer rebellion.

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:03.200
I'm well-researched on it,
but, I haven't made it yet.

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:04.033
But,

00:23:04.033 --> 00:23:06.310
- In that case, we're previewing
that it's coming contents

00:23:06.310 --> 00:23:07.287
that people

00:23:07.287 --> 00:23:09.740
- It's been up there for a while.

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:11.790
I gotta be careful what I write up there.

00:23:13.232 --> 00:23:15.450
So, the next, next
question was maybe almost,

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:18.240
almost asymmetric opposite of that.

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:19.810
Well, maybe it's related from Facebook,

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:23.440
Surabi Srivastava says,
how to manage a teenager

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:26.020
who's taking the quarantine
like fish to water,

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:27.730
need to break him away from the screen

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:28.730
with very limited choices.

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:31.670
So this is a parent who's
worried because it's maybe

00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:34.723
their child really likes this COVID world.

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:37.120
- It is, we're, we're hearing that,

00:23:37.120 --> 00:23:39.450
I'm sorry, my reception is jumping again,

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:42.300
but we're, we're hearing that
a lot from teenage parents

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:45.440
is that we get one of two
concerns in one of two directions.

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:48.560
It's either I'm worried
about how my teenager's

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:50.750
gonna cope with the lack of, you know,

00:23:50.750 --> 00:23:51.810
connection with friends.

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:52.643
How do I deal with that?

00:23:52.643 --> 00:23:54.280
And that kind of references the answer

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:55.820
I gave to an earlier question.

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:58.520
And then there's the, they're
not coming out of their room

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:01.550
like, you know, do you, do
you think they're depressed

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:04.170
or could it be that
they're just, you know,

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:05.950
not liking time spent with their family

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:07.610
or they're liking this too much

00:24:07.610 --> 00:24:09.270
and all our possible conclusions,

00:24:09.270 --> 00:24:11.050
you know, we've helped many a parent

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:13.190
to just kind of think through the fact

00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:15.390
that your teenager
isn't, is in their room,

00:24:15.390 --> 00:24:17.390
may not necessarily mean their mood is low

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:19.470
or they've experienced any
disruption in their eating

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:21.013
or sleeping patterns or you know,

00:24:21.013 --> 00:24:22.870
that they're feeling all hopeless.

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:25.835
They may just be enjoying
themselves like a pig in mud,

00:24:25.835 --> 00:24:27.090
in that sense.

00:24:27.090 --> 00:24:29.790
So you know, when, when
we see this kind of thing

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:31.810
with teenagers, we've
tried to talk parents

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:35.050
through two kind of major strategies.

00:24:35.050 --> 00:24:38.900
One is just kind of trying to
get to their non-negotiables.

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:41.980
You know, when do you want your
teenager out of their room,

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:44.940
family meals, at least an hour a day.

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:46.950
Maybe they gotta take a walk outside

00:24:46.950 --> 00:24:49.840
cause you have decreed they
have to get some exercise,

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:52.017
while practicing social distancing.

00:24:52.017 --> 00:24:53.630
You know, we get those non negotiables.

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:56.540
And then the other thing,
we've been coaching parents on

00:24:56.540 --> 00:24:59.670
is strategies to keep the
teenager out of their room

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:01.740
when they spontaneously emerge.

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:04.450
So in that sense, we try
to coach teenage parents

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:07.492
or parents of teenagers on, you know,

00:25:07.492 --> 00:25:10.220
trying not to pepper
the kiddo with questions

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:11.490
the moment they walk out,

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:13.410
use declarative statements,

00:25:13.410 --> 00:25:15.560
praise something you
know, they might've done.

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:17.570
Like, it really seemed
like you were working

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:20.090
on your English essay hard earlier today,

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:22.810
or hey, you know, I'm
making spaghetti sauce,

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:25.660
you always know how to make it just right.

00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:27.460
You know, it's that you're trying to draw

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:29.300
them out with skills they have,

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:32.780
value to the family, you
know, and conversation,

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:35.720
rather than, did you get
your schoolwork done today?

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:37.990
You know, what else was on your list,

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:41.113
which inevitably sends a
teenager back into their room.

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:46.200
- Yeah, I really liked that last point.

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:47.807
You know, a lot of times I always say,

00:25:47.807 --> 00:25:51.170
and this isn't based on research,
but more just gut senses.

00:25:51.170 --> 00:25:53.780
You know, teenagers,
especially our prodo adults,

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:56.880
you know, biologically the
nature would allow them

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:58.640
to be even be parents, not recommending it

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:00.160
by any stretch of the imagination,

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:02.320
but that implies that
they are psychologically

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:05.330
and biologically capable
of caring for themselves

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:06.240
and others.

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:08.720
But modern society has
kind of just kept them

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:10.150
only worrying about themselves.

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:12.850
And your last point about
helping with the pasta,

00:26:12.850 --> 00:26:14.500
it's really like, hey, let's make,

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:15.960
I'm gonna treat you like an adult

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:17.580
or a pro prodo adult or pre adult.

00:26:17.580 --> 00:26:19.150
I'm gonna give you some responsibility

00:26:19.150 --> 00:26:20.450
for the household as well.

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:22.530
- I think what you're highlighting

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:27.530
is the intersection of
resources, socio-economic status,

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:31.340
cultural background and maturity

00:26:31.340 --> 00:26:32.674
in the sense that, you know,

00:26:32.674 --> 00:26:36.040
in within kind of American culture,

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:39.020
we have sort of a period
of extended adolescence

00:26:39.020 --> 00:26:41.593
in which we've really very gradually

00:26:41.593 --> 00:26:44.350
ask teenagers who are seeking novelty,

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:48.558
or seeking new experiences,
to take on responsibilities.

00:26:48.558 --> 00:26:51.200
Now that is very much converging

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:54.150
with the fact that if
parents are experiencing

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:57.280
job insecurity during this
time or they're overwhelmed

00:26:57.280 --> 00:26:59.920
for childcare, we're
often asking teenagers

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:02.130
to take on a larger share
of their responsibility

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:04.550
In a crisis like this one or when someone

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:08.940
is confronting that kind of, you know,

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:10.700
situation just in general.

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:14.150
So it really, you know,
the question of a teenager

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:17.370
takes to COVID a little bit
too much, usually entails

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:19.540
the fact that that teenager's
main responsibility

00:27:19.540 --> 00:27:22.110
prior to this was perhaps school.

00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:25.470
And you know, that in some sense it's okay

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:27.670
if we're a little bit
stricter about the idea

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:30.579
that we need some help during this time.

00:27:30.579 --> 00:27:32.850
And it may be, it may behoove them

00:27:32.850 --> 00:27:35.230
to kind of serve the
family a little bit better

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:38.290
than they might have
otherwise since their day job,

00:27:38.290 --> 00:27:42.040
school has sort of shifted this.

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:42.890
- Yeah, and I think we have time

00:27:42.890 --> 00:27:43.990
for maybe one more question.

00:27:43.990 --> 00:27:46.443
There's a couple of questions
that are in this category.

00:27:46.443 --> 00:27:50.070
This is, from several folks

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:52.260
who are talking about feeling stressed,

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:54.350
either when they're trying
to learn a specific thing

00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:57.360
like math or reading or feeling stressed

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:59.300
because they actually have
a fairly large to do list

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:00.950
from school and they're feeling

00:28:00.950 --> 00:28:02.110
like they're not able to do it.

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:03.524
What tips do you have to those students

00:28:03.524 --> 00:28:04.963
who are feeling stressed?

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:08.140
- Yeah, I mean, part of it is that I think

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:10.800
everybody's managing this crisis together.

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:13.980
So more so than ever, it both feels alone,

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:15.380
but you're not alone.

00:28:15.380 --> 00:28:17.900
So, you know, the reality is we're hearing

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:19.490
from so many students who will tell us

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:22.410
I can't manage the workload
or I feel so ashamed

00:28:22.410 --> 00:28:24.690
because the other
students seem to be taking

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:26.700
to this online learning
like a fish out of water,

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:27.830
but not me.

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:30.170
And I don't, we don't really meet people

00:28:30.170 --> 00:28:32.480
who are finding it easy during this time.

00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:35.240
So I think it's more that
we wanna encourage people

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:37.040
to reach out to their fellow students

00:28:37.040 --> 00:28:38.390
for how they're coping with this

00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:40.020
and to reach out to their teachers

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:42.730
because we're all, I think, you know,

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:44.960
functioning on more limited time

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:48.070
and, kind of head space
than we normally have.

00:28:48.070 --> 00:28:50.150
But at the same time, we wanna help.

00:28:50.150 --> 00:28:53.030
Like we know that a switch from in-person

00:28:53.030 --> 00:28:55.700
to online schooling will
not come without bumps.

00:28:55.700 --> 00:28:57.990
And so for that reason we've seen teachers

00:28:57.990 --> 00:29:00.100
more so than ever, you know, interested

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:02.280
in connecting really quick individually

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:04.220
to try to help a student
through something.

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:06.090
We're figuring out how
they can get support.

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:08.230
And I think that a lot of teenagers we see

00:29:08.230 --> 00:29:11.430
and college students are
really surprised by that.

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:12.530
- Yeah, and I'll just add to that,

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:13.900
because we've been seeing
that type of stress,

00:29:13.900 --> 00:29:15.690
not just among students
using Khan Academy,

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:18.530
but we've been seeing it
even in our own employee base

00:29:18.530 --> 00:29:19.890
where you know, folks have, you know,

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:21.010
their head space has shrunk.

00:29:21.010 --> 00:29:24.360
There's a baseline of
anxiety or dread going on

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:26.250
and then they're taking care of kids.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:29.030
They have a, might have a,
a family member who's having

00:29:29.030 --> 00:29:30.600
financial difficulty cause they lost.

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:32.830
I mean there's just all sorts
of stresses that are going on.

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:35.100
And then they're also
trying to do their work

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:37.070
and they're feeling
almost guilt or stress.

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:38.420
They're like, Oh my God, the goals

00:29:38.420 --> 00:29:39.970
that I thought I was gonna be able to do

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:41.490
are not realistic anymore.

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:42.870
And what we're telling our team members,

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:43.970
and what we tell everyone

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:46.310
is no one is judging anyone right now.

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:49.620
Anyway, I only judge the
people who are judging others,

00:29:49.620 --> 00:29:50.980
right now (laughs)

00:29:50.980 --> 00:29:53.960
- We keep telling folks,
you take bite sized chunks

00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:56.810
and then you do it in this
as high quality as you can,

00:29:56.810 --> 00:29:59.840
but don't think about quantity
or the same productivity

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:01.540
right now, no one's capable of

00:30:02.510 --> 00:30:04.330
- Right, and no one is judging it,

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:06.080
and you just have to release yourself.

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:07.560
I think, for example, a lot of schools,

00:30:07.560 --> 00:30:08.760
even my own children's school,

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:11.040
they've given a very robust list of things

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:12.480
for the students to do.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:15.470
But, I think I can speak on their behalf.

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:19.140
They completely get that
if kids or their parents

00:30:19.140 --> 00:30:20.830
are not in a position to keep the kids

00:30:20.830 --> 00:30:22.500
to do everything on that checklist,

00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:24.260
it's completely okay.

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:26.240
We're all trying to
cope with this situation

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:27.980
in whatever way we can.

00:30:27.980 --> 00:30:30.140
So, Dave, thanks so much for joining.

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:31.470
We're at time every day.

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:33.220
This, you know, this
half an hour goes by fast

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:34.560
and today was even faster than normal.

00:30:34.560 --> 00:30:36.610
A sign of how much fun we're having.

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:38.280
I think you're gonna be able to join us

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:40.960
hopefully in future live streams.

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:44.520
And I just wanna tell everyone
as what Dave and I just said,

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:47.770
you know, you might feel
physically separated from others

00:30:47.770 --> 00:30:49.870
and Dave gave some good
ideas for how you might

00:30:49.870 --> 00:30:51.788
feel a little bit mentally closer,

00:30:51.788 --> 00:30:55.440
or emotionally closer to
others in this time of crisis.

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:57.630
And the whole point of this live stream

00:30:57.630 --> 00:30:58.780
is for all of us to realize

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:00.260
that we're in this together.

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:02.150
The whole world is going through this.

00:31:02.150 --> 00:31:06.790
So as best as you can, you
know, keep safe and stay healthy

00:31:06.790 --> 00:31:08.580
and don't beat up yourself too much,

00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:11.070
and take some time to breathe, relax,

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:13.140
meditate, whatever it
takes to kind of give

00:31:13.140 --> 00:31:15.380
yourself that mental reset.

00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:16.950
We'll see you tomorrow.

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:18.057
- Brilliant advice.

